<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/documents.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/documents-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/documents.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.15"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Claim glossary</span></nav><p class="kicker">Reference</p><h1>Kansas Property Claim Glossary</h1><p class="lede">42 terms you’ll meet in policies, estimates and appraisal awards, defined in plain English. Type in the box to filter.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><div class="filter"><label for="gloss-filter">Search the glossary</label><input id="gloss-filter" type="search" placeholder="Try “schedule” or “umpire”" autocomplete="off"><p class="hint" data-gloss-count>42 terms</p></div><div class="az"><a href="#g-A">A</a><a href="#g-C">C</a><a href="#g-D">D</a><a href="#g-E">E</a><a href="#g-F">F</a><a href="#g-H">H</a><a href="#g-I">I</a><a href="#g-K">K</a><a href="#g-L">L</a><a href="#g-M">M</a><a href="#g-O">O</a><a href="#g-P">P</a><a href="#g-R">R</a><a href="#g-S">S</a><a href="#g-T">T</a><a href="#g-U">U</a><a href="#g-W">W</a></div><dl class="glossary"><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-A">Actual cash value (ACV)</dt><dd>Replacement cost minus depreciation. The first payment under most replacement cost policies.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Additional living expense (ALE)</dt><dd>Coverage for extra costs of living elsewhere while a home is repaired after a covered loss.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Appraisal award</dt><dd>The written decision, signed by two of the three panel members, that sets the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Appraisal clause</dt><dd>The policy condition that lets either party demand appraisal when they disagree on the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Appraiser</dt><dd>A competent, impartial person chosen by each side to value the loss in appraisal.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-C">Coinsurance</dt><dd>A commercial policy condition that can reduce payment if a building is insured for less than a required percentage of its value.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Cosmetic damage exclusion</dt><dd>An endorsement that excludes hail dents or marks that don’t affect a roof’s function, common on metal roofs.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Coverage A</dt><dd>Dwelling coverage for the house itself. Percentage deductibles are often based on this limit.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Coverage B</dt><dd>Other structures coverage, such as detached garages, sheds and fences.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Coverage C</dt><dd>Personal property, or contents, coverage.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-D">Debris removal</dt><dd>Coverage for removing debris from covered damage, often subject to a separate limit.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Declarations page</dt><dd>The summary page of a policy listing coverages, limits, deductibles and endorsements.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Demand for appraisal</dt><dd>The written notice that invokes the appraisal clause.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Depreciation</dt><dd>The reduction in value for age and condition. Recoverable depreciation can be paid after repairs.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Derecho</dt><dd>A long-lived, widespread windstorm with damaging straight-line winds.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Drip edge</dt><dd>Metal flashing along roof edges, often required by code and frequently missed in estimates.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-E">Endorsement</dt><dd>A form that adds to or changes the terms of a policy.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Estimate</dt><dd>A line-item list of repair quantities and prices, usually prepared in estimating software.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-F">FAIR Plan</dt><dd>A state residual market for basic property insurance. Kansas has the Kansas FAIR Plan.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Functional damage</dt><dd>Damage that affects how a roof or component performs, as opposed to appearance only.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-H">Hail strike</dt><dd>An impact mark from hail, counted in test squares to assess roof damage.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Holdback</dt><dd>Recoverable depreciation withheld until repairs are complete.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-I">Ice-and-water shield</dt><dd>A self-adhering roof underlayment used at eaves and valleys to resist ice dams.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-K">Kansas Insurance Department</dt><dd>The state agency in Topeka that regulates insurers and helps consumers.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-L">Line item</dt><dd>A single entry in an estimate, such as a roofing square of shingles or a linear foot of gutter.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-M">Matching</dt><dd>The issue of whether repaired areas can reasonably match undamaged areas in color, texture or profile.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Mitigation</dt><dd>Reasonable steps to prevent further damage after a loss, such as tarping or drying.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-O">Ordinance or law</dt><dd>Coverage for added costs to meet current building codes during repairs.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Overhead and profit (O&amp;P)</dt><dd>A general contractor’s charges for overhead and profit, often 10% and 10%.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-P">Payment schedule (roof)</dt><dd>An endorsement that pays a percentage of roof replacement cost based on roof age.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Percentage deductible</dt><dd>A deductible calculated as a percentage of the dwelling limit, common for wind and hail.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Pitch</dt><dd>Roof slope expressed as rise per 12 inches of run, such as 6/12.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Proof of loss</dt><dd>A sworn statement of the amount claimed, sometimes requested by the insurer.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-R">Replacement cost value (RCV)</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace with materials of like kind and quality, without depreciation.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Roofing square</dt><dd>One hundred square feet of roof surface.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-S">Scope</dt><dd>The list of damaged items and the repairs needed.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Steep charge</dt><dd>An added labor charge for roofs with steeper pitches, often 7/12 and above.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Suit limitation</dt><dd>The policy clause that sets a time limit for suing the insurer.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Supplement</dt><dd>A request for additional payment for items missed or under-priced in the original estimate.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-T">Test square</dt><dd>A 10-by-10-foot area on a roof slope used to count hail strikes.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-U">Umpire</dt><dd>The neutral third member of an appraisal panel who decides items the appraisers can’t agree on.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-W">Wind/hail deductible</dt><dd>A separate deductible that applies to wind and hail losses, often a percentage.</dd></div></dl></article>
